Yes. In a DataGenerator, `STOP` is the normal way to finish generation successfully. It is **not** an error signal.

A typical pattern is:

```ctl
//#CTL2
integer counter = 0;

function integer generate() {
    if (counter >= 10) {
        return STOP;   // successful completion: no more records to generate
    }

    $out.0.id    = counter;
    $out.0.label = "Record_" + num2str(counter);
    counter++;

    return OK;         // emit this record and call generate() again
}
```

**Return values from `generate()`:**

| Return | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `OK` | Emit `$out.0` as a record and call `generate()` again |
| `SKIP` | Do not emit a record and call `generate()` again |
| `STOP` | Finish generation successfully |

`STOP` means the generator is done. Use error handling mechanisms only for actual failures.

Also remember that DataGenerator has **no input port**, so `generate()` works with `$out.0` and globals, not `$in.0`.

