I tried to throttle a DataGenerator using today() and sleep, but it still bursts records. Here's what I did:

long last = 0L;

function integer generate() {
    date nowDate = today();
    long now = date2long(nowDate);

    if (last > 0L && now - last < 500L) {
        sleep(500L);
    }

    last = now;
    $out.0.id = sequence(MySeq).next();
    return OK;
}

Why is it still sending records too fast? How should I fix this?

The issue is that you always sleep for the full 500 ms instead of just the missing time. If `now - last` is, say, 450 ms, you only need to wait 50 ms, not 500. Also, you don't need `today()` and `date2long()` here; `getCurrentTimeMillis()` is simpler and more precise.

A better version:

long last = 0L;

function integer generate() {
    long now = getCurrentTimeMillis();
    long minGap = 500L;

    if (last > 0L) {
        long delta = now - last;
        if (delta < minGap) {
            sleep(minGap - delta);
            now = getCurrentTimeMillis();  // refresh after sleep
        }
    }

    last = now;
    $out.0.id = sequence(MySeq).next();
    return OK;
}

This sleeps only for the remaining time needed to reach your 500 ms interval, resulting in a steadier rate.

