    return OK;
}

function integer transformOnError(string errorMessage, string stackTrace) {
    return SKIP;
}
```

Use **DataGenerator**. DataGenerator has no input port -- it creates records from code with no upstream data source.

```ctl
//#CTL2
integer counter = 0;

function integer generate() {
    counter++;
    if (counter > 100) return STOP;   // STOP signals normal termination

    $out.0.id    = counter;
    $out.0.label = "Record_" + num2str(counter);
    return OK;
}
```

`generate()` is called repeatedly by the component. Each call that returns `OK` emits one record. When it returns `STOP`, the component shuts down normally.

**Why not Map?** Map has an input port -- it processes records that come from an upstream component. If there is no upstream data source, Map has nothing to call `transform()` with. DataGenerator is the only component that operates without input.

**DataGenerator vs Normalizer for generation:**
- **DataGenerator**: no input; generates entirely from code (counters, constants, sequences, random values)
- **Normalizer**: has input; expands each input record into multiple output records

If you have a small "seed" file that drives the generation (e.g. a list of categories and you want N records per category), use a Normalizer fed from the seed file -- `count()` returns N and `transform(idx)` generates each record. If there is no input at all, use DataGenerator.
